<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phormio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phormio was an Athenian admiral of the 5th century BCE. Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> he commanded a squadron based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> and in 429 BCE won two notable naval victories over larger Peloponnesian fleets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>. He also campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>, whose people became allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Phormio was the son of Asopius. He first appears in 440 BCE, when he shared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagnon">Hagnon</a> and others the command of the Athenian fleet in the later part of the Samian War. In 432 BCE he led 1,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> to the siege of Potidaea, approached slowly from the side the Athenians had not yet surrounded, and built a counter-wall that completed the city's investment. He then campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>' enemies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, and the next year attacked the Chalcidians again alongside Perdiccas II, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent to the Corinthian Gulf with 20 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, he based himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> and blockaded Corinthian shipping. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> prepared a fleet and army to overrun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>, take Zacynthus and Cephallenia and perhaps Naupactus itself. When the Peloponnesian fleet of 47 ships, many loaded with heavy infantry, tried to cross from the south shore to the north in open water, it formed a defensive circle with prows outward. Phormio's ships circled it, driving it tighter at the risk of being rammed in the flank, until a wind made the inexperienced crews foul their oars; the Athenians then routed them and seized 12 ships.</p><p>Shortly afterwards he faced 77 Peloponnesian ships in the narrow waters of the gulf. Drawn in to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a>, the Athenians were at first routed and divided, but 11 ships pursued into Naupactus turned on their pursuers and won. The victory preserved Athenian naval supremacy in the gulf and ended Peloponnesian attempts to challenge it in that period of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a land campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a> in 428 BCE he is not recorded in command again. A defeat in the gulf would have struck hard at Athenian influence in the north-east and at the city's name for naval invincibility. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> erected a statue of him on the acropolis and buried him in the state cemetery, and he is counted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a> among its great admirals. His son, also named Asopius, commanded a naval expedition during the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phormio” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hostility to Corinth's colonies in the region pushed them into alliance with Athens, whose general <b>Phormio</b> campaigned with them in the Peloponnesian War.</blockquote><a href="/state/acarnanian-league">Acarnanian League</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-435" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Phormio"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-435">What was here in 435 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
